Initial current:
\[
I_0=\frac{10}{2.0\times10^3}=5.0\times10^{-3}\,\mathrm{A}
=5.0\,\mathrm{mA}
\]
Time constant:
\[
\tau=RC=(2.0\times10^3)(100\times10^{-6})=0.20\,\mathrm{s}
\]
At \(t=0.20\,\mathrm{s}=\tau\),
\[
V_C=10(1-e^{-1})=6.32\,\mathrm{V}
\]

For a first-order RC circuit, the capacitor voltage moves exponentially from its initial value to its final value:
\[
V_C(t)=V_f+(V_i-V_f)e^{-t/\tau}
\]
Here \(V_i=5.0\,\mathrm{V}\) and \(V_f=12\,\mathrm{V}\), so
\[
V_C(t)=12+(5.0-12)e^{-t/\tau}
=12-7.0e^{-t/\tau}
\]
